From a7747d805895f13d690b283dd891a10447e5e52e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Upstream Port <upstream-port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:00:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 81/81] video/mt6768: port the display-side M4U client glue,
 without ION

The M4U engine (0080) has an entry point every media client uses -
m4u_alloc_mva(client, port, va, sg_table, size, prot, flags, *pMva) - but nothing in
this tree had ever called it.  This commit lands the first such caller, chosen by
tracing rather than by directory size, and it is the client half only: 10 files,
1,116 lines under drivers/misc/mediatek/video/ plus one source line each in
drivers/misc/{Kconfig,Makefile}, 2 objects, no device tree edit.

Why this file and not the panel path.  drivers/misc/mediatek/video in the 4.19 tree is
647,104 lines of C (71,401 for mt6768 alone: dispsys 34,419 + videox 36,982, plus 10,328
of shared IP blocks).  Stock's build shape, read off even_defconfig and the vendor
Makefiles: CONFIG_MTK_FB=y and CONFIG_MTK_LCM=y, so video/Makefile:32
(ifneq $(CONFIG_MTK_LCM),y) excludes mtdummy/ entirely and common/Makefile:103
(ifneq $(CONFIG_MTK_FB),y) excludes common/mtkfb_dummy.o; CONFIG_MTK_VIDEOX, by contrast,
appears only in video/Kconfig and in zero Makefiles, so it gates nothing and the legacy
videox path is built after all.  Inside that subset, exactly one chain reaches the MMU
for a buffer without needing ION, and it is the boot-visible one:

  videox/mtkfb.c:2648            disp_hal_allocate_framebuffer(fb_base, fb_base+vramsize-1)
    ddp_m4u.c:338                  ioremap_wc(pa, len); sg_alloc_table(sgt, 1);
                                   sg_dma_address/len = pa/len; m4u_create_client();
                                   m4u_alloc_mva(client, DISP_M4U_PORT_DISP_OVL0, 0, sgt,
                                                 size, M4U_PROT_READ|WRITE, 0, &mva)
  videox/primary_display.c:4113  config_display_m4u_port() -> m4u_config_port() x4,
                                 Virtuality=1 Security=0 Distance=1 Direction=0
  dispsys/ddp_drv.c:557          disp_m4u_init() -> m4u_register_fault_callback() x4
  dispsys/ddp_drv.c:593          disp_helper_option_init() -> DISP_OPT_USE_M4U = 1

Ported, then, are ddp_m4u.c/.h (249 of the vendor's 400 lines, ddp_m4u.h 66 of 72), ddp_hal.h verbatim
(140, it owns enum DISP_MODULE_ENUM) and the option machinery of videox/disp_helper.c
(452 of 453) that the file reads.  ddp_m4u.o's five M4U references resolve into the ported
driver: m4u_alloc_mva/m4u_create_client/m4u_mva_map_kernel into m4u/2.0/m4u.o,
m4u_config_port/m4u_register_fault_callback into m4u/mt6768/m4u_hw.o.

No ION, and no dma-buf heaps either - deliberately, and in the vendor's own direction.
All 41 ion_ references in ddp_m4u.c sit in the seven disp_ion_*() wrappers, and every body
is guarded by #if defined(MTK_FB_ION_SUPPORT), a macro that appears in no Kconfig and no
Makefile of the tree (it comes from the Android userspace build), so stock's kernel build
compiled none of it either.  The wrappers are removed together with their prototypes
because those need struct ion_client, struct ion_handle and enum ION_CACHE_SYNC_TYPE from
headers v5.15 has no source for; nothing in this tree references them.  A client that
insists on ION_CMD_MULTIMEDIA booking is a decision for the user, not a dependency to
smuggle in (see report/m4u-ion-audit.md section 8).

Four dependencies were resolved by leaving them out, each with the reason in the file:
  - ddp_reg.h pulls display_recorder.h, cmdq_record.h, cmdq_core.h and 8 ddp_reg_*.h; the
    only thing ddp_m4u.c wants from it is the USE_M4U=0 fallback that clears MMU_EN in
    SMI_LARB0_NON_SEC_CON+0..12.  Those registers belong to drivers/memory/mtk-smi here
    (0078/0079), so the client no longer pokes them - it logs instead.  Worth recording:
    that branch is not dead upstream, because ddp_drv.c:557 runs disp_m4u_init() before
    ddp_drv.c:593 sets the option, and disp_helper.c:71 still defaults
    DISP_OPT_USE_M4U to 0 ("must enable"), so stock does clear those four bits at probe.
  - ddp_dump.c (1,643 lines of register reads through the same layer) supplied the fault
    callback's dump calls; the callback keeps its log and the module resolution.
  - ddp_info.c's ddp_get_module_name() needs the ddp_modules[] register-info table, so the
    error path prints the enum value instead of the name.
  - the vendor log layer (ddp_log.h and disp_drv_log.h) mirrors every message through
    dprec_logger_pr() in display_recorder.c and gates some levels on ddp_debug.c, so this
    tree gets a 51-line ddp_log.h that maps DDP*/DISP* onto the vendor's fallback arms
    (pr_err/pr_info/pr_debug).  /dev/pmsg/dprec recording of display M4U events is
    unavailable; DISPINFO/DISPMSG/DISPCHECK stay pr_debug as on stock.
In disp_helper.c, the DynFPS hook (primary_fps_ctx_set_wnd_sz, from primary_display.c) is
removed from disp_helper_set_option(), and the FAKE_LCM_WIDTH/HEIGHT cases - which call
primary_display_get_virtual_width()/DISP_GetScreenWidth() - are guarded by CONFIG_MTK_FB,
the symbol that gates videox, so they read the table (0 = no fake LCM) until it lands.
disp_global_stage still initialises to DISP_HELPER_STAGE_NORMAL because
CONFIG_FPGA_EARLY_PORTING is unset, as on stock.

Gated, not wired.  CONFIG_MTK_DISP_M4U (default y, obj-y, no probe, no initcall) is
deliberate: config_display_m4u_port() switches four display ports to virtual addressing
while LK keeps scanning the logo out of physical addresses, and the panel/DPM core that
owns the rest of that sequence is not here.  So no node may be bound by this patch and
none is - the client has no of_device_id table.

Verification, build-37 (arm64 defconfig + this port's config recipe, clang/lld):
  - 0 compiler errors, 0 make failures, 0 undefined references, 0 new warnings; 7,379
    objects (build-36: 7,377, +exactly the two new ones), 840 .ko, both objects obj-y.
  - System.map carries all twelve client symbols (module_to_m4u_port, module_to_m4u_larb,
    m4u_port_to_module, disp_m4u_init, disp_m4u_callback, config_display_m4u_port,
    disp_mva_map_kernel, disp_mva_unmap_kernel, disp_allocate_mva,
    disp_hal_allocate_framebuffer, disp_helper_get_option, disp_helper_option_init).
  - hwenable.py against the built tree, same method as before and without --compat-index:
    450 compatible-bearing nodes, 349 distinct, 34 bound / 25 enabled / 5 enableable / 315
    driverless, and the row-by-row diff against build-36 is EMPTY.  mediatek,m4u remains
    ENABLED, mediatek,dispsys and mediatek,mtkfb remain NO_DRIVER as they must.
  - clkaudit.py --require-fresh unchanged: 234 refs / 234 registered / 0 unresolved.
  - mt6768.dtb 122,474 B sha256 34a7e6b5...a11cd, unchanged again: this commit does not
    touch a device tree, and dtbo.img rebuilds byte-identical (38fe681f...).
  - Image 27,035,656 B - the same size as build-36 - with 6 KB of new text inside it,
    absorbed by section padding; Image.gz 10,647,924 B, Image.gz-dtb 11,141,441 B with the
    identical 493,517-byte 6-blob DTB payload.
Runtime evidence, host only: upstream-port/tests/ compiles these same two .c files for the
host against the real ported m4u_port.h + m4u_v2.h + m4u_v2_ext.h and a recording M4U stub,
then runs the whole traced sequence.  43 checks, 0 failures.  It confirms USE_M4U reads 0
before disp_helper_option_init() and 1 after; four fault callbacks on ports 0..3; four
m4u_config_port() calls with Virtuality=1; the fb handover passing one sg entry with
dma_address=pa and length=size, prot READ|WRITE, flags 0; sg_alloc_table failure returning
-ENOMEM with no M4U call at all; and vunmap() on a page-aligned kernel VA.  It also pins two
semantics that are easy to get wrong later: struct m4u_port_config_struct.domain is left
uninitialised by the client (only harmless because MT6768 has one domain and m4u.c derives it
from the port), and the MVA the client pre-sets into *pMva is not honoured, because
M4U_FLAGS_FIX_MVA is not passed - the returned MVA is the one to program.  The same probe
compiled against the 4.19 vendor headers is identical: M4U_PORT_NR=61, ports 0/1/2/3,
sizeof(struct m4u_port_config_struct)=24 with Virtuality@4 domain@12 Distance@16
Direction@20, M4U_PROT_READ=1 WRITE=2, M4U_FLAGS_FIX_MVA=2 SG_READY=16.

Readiness, stated in four parts so the next reader is not misled: source ported and
build+link verified; DT-binding verification done and negative; runtime verified on the host
against a stub only, with no MMIO, translation, SMI/LARB or IRQ behaviour observed; not
flashed, not booted, no display function, and no emulator available in this sandbox
(qemu-system-aarch64 is not installable here).  Detail: report/display-m4u-client.md,
KNOWN-ISSUES.md 12, report/display-m4u-client.json.
